bb_pair: persist the paired peer into the on-disk baseband config

bb_pair's pairing dispatch (PRJ_CMD_EVENT_PAIR via BB_SET_PRJ_DISPATCH)
only ever changes the chip's live/RAM state. S60ar8030/S97ar8030 push
the on-disk fw_name=/cfg_name= config fresh into the chip at every
modprobe, unconditionally -- so a successful pair is silently undone
on the very next reboot, and has to be redone from scratch every time.

After the existing pairing dispatch completes, poll BB_GET_STATUS (same
"is this slot real" check ar8030-status.c already uses: valid
bb_link_state_e, non-zero peer MAC) for the resulting peer, then:

  - write it into the on-disk config so it survives the next reboot's
    firmware push -- AP role gets a single-entry candidate allowlist at
    baseband.basic.ap.candidate.{slot,slotN} (matches whichever form the
    file already uses -- see usr_master.json/usr_slave.json/
    bb_config_director.json for the slotN form), DEV role gets
    baseband.basic.dev.ap_mac. Same AP/DEV split upstream's own (unused)
    dev_helper/bb_pair/bb_pair.cpp reference already uses for the
    equivalent live-only BB_SET_CANDIDATES/BB_SET_AP_MAC calls.
  - best-effort apply the same change live via BB_SET_CANDIDATES/
    BB_SET_AP_MAC too, so the current session doesn't itself need a
    reboot -- not fatal if the running firmware ignores it, since the
    file is what actually matters after a reboot.

-c/--cfg overrides the config path (default: /lib/firmware/ar8030/
bb_config_gnd_pro.json, matching this package's default RF-board
variant -- see files/etc/default/ar8030's own BB_CFG for the same
"hardcoded to the reference unit, override if yours differs" caveat);
--no-persist keeps the old pair-only behavior.

Needs a JSON library that doesn't otherwise exist in this SDK (the
config file is pushed as an opaque blob to the chip's own RTOS, which
does its own parsing) -- added via pkg-config against buildroot's cjson
package rather than vendoring one.

Ported verbatim from the OpenIPC/builder air-side ar8030 package's
identical patch -- keep the two in sync.

Not yet verified against real hardware: whether the peer MAC actually
appears in BB_GET_STATUS within the polling window in practice, and
whether the running firmware acts on the live BB_SET_CANDIDATES/
BB_SET_AP_MAC calls at all (best-effort, not required for the on-disk
persistence to work).

